<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suceava</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suceava is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, in the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>. It was the capital of the Principality of Moldavia from about 1388 until 1564, when the court moved to Iași, and its fortress withstood an Ottoman siege under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> in 1476. It belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from 1775 to 1918 and is surrounded by the painted monasteries of Bukovina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suceava.jpg" alt="Suceava" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rosenborg BK Fan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palatul_Administrativ_din_Suceava_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Suceava is the seat of Suceava County, in the historical regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> and Western Moldavia. It stands in a moderately hilly area in the south-western part of the county and covers both hills, the highest being Zamca Hill, and the meadows of the Suceava river valley. Its position at the crossroads of several trade routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> made it a commercial town, and it is a transport hub with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> to the west. The name was explained by the chroniclers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigore-ureche">Grigore Ureche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimitrie-cantemir">Dimitrie Cantemir</a> as coming from the Hungarian Szűcsvár, meaning furriers' castle, although there is no historical or vernacular evidence for this.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was inhabited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> period, and traces of a Dacian oppidum of the 2nd century survive. Suceava and the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> were not conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> and remained among the lands of the free Dacian tribes. Ptolemy places the Celtic-speaking Anartes and Taurisci and the Germanic Bastarnae in the region. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the population was invaded in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>.</p><p>From 1388 to 1564 Suceava was the capital of the Principality of Moldavia, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it operated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petru-i-of-moldavia">Petru Mușat</a> built the Princely House in wood in the late 14th century, and after 1400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-good">Alexander I of Moldavia</a> rebuilt it and added a stone wall and stone buildings. A fire in the second half of the 15th century burned the wooden house. The Seat Fortress and the Șcheia Fortress belonged to the principality's system of fortification.</p><p>From 1775 to 1918 Suceava was under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. The Austrian census of 1880 recorded 10,104 inhabitants, of whom 5,862 were Bukovina Germans and 2,652 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>. In the interwar period it grew within the enlarged Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Suceava had a population of 84,308 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, having grown from just over 10,000 in the late 1940s to over 100,000 in the early 1990s. The reconstructed Seat Fortress stands on the eastern edge of the city. The Saint John the New Monastery, whose Church of Saint George was built between 1514 and 1522 and served as the metropolitan cathedral of Moldavia until 1677, is one of the Churches of Moldavia inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. An equestrian statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a>, made in 1977, is the tallest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. The Ștefan cel Mare University of Suceava was established in 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suceava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors, Lațcu and Petru I Mușat, built the institutions of a state: Lațcu sought a Catholic bishopric at Siret around 1371, while Petru I struck coins, did homage to Władysław II Jagiełło of Poland at Lviv in 1387 to balance Hungary, and established the court at <b>Suceava</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orthodox metropolitan resided in Czernowitz, and the painted monasteries of <b>Suceava</b> were preserved.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bukovina">Duchy of Bukovina</a></li></ul></section>
